Overall, you've done a good job converting the controller to use flash messages. 
I'd give it 8.5/10 right now. There are a few important corrections to make before you continue 
with the rest of the app.

1. ❌ res.redirect() does not accept a second argument
You have this in several places:

return res.redirect("/auth/login", {
    pageTitle: "Login"
});
and

return res.redirect("/auth/contact", {
    pageTitle: "Contact"
});
This is incorrect.

res.redirect() only accepts the URL (and optionally a status code).

It should simply be:

return res.redirect("/auth/login");
and

return res.redirect("/auth/contact");

2. ❌ Login failures should use flash
Instead of:

return res.render("failureLogin", {
    pageTitle: "Failure Login",
    message: "Invalid username/email or password."
});
use:

req.flash(
    "error",
    "Invalid username/email or password."
);

return res.redirect("/auth/login");
Do this for:

missing credentials

user not found

password mismatch

3. ❌ Signup failures
Instead of:

return res.render("failure", ...)
use:

req.flash(
    "error",
    "Passwords do not match."
);

return res.redirect("/auth/signup");
Likewise:

req.flash(
    "error",
    "User already exists."
);

return res.redirect("/auth/signup");
4. ❌ Signup success message
You currently have:

req.flash(
    "success",
    "User updated successfully."
);
That message is wrong.

It should be:

req.flash(
    "success",
    "Account created successfully. Please log in."
);
5. ⚠ Logout
This is the biggest issue.

You have:

req.session.destroy((err) => {

    req.flash(
        "success",
        "Logout successful."
    );

    res.redirect("/");
});
After

req.session.destroy()
the session is gone.

connect-flash stores messages in the session, so the flash may not survive.

A safer approach is to set the flash before destroying the session, 
or regenerate the session after logout if needed. Depending on your session store, 
the current code may not reliably display the logout message.

6. ❌ You're inventing flash keys
Your middleware exposes only:

success
error
warning
But later you do:

req.flash("failureMessage", ...)
req.flash("failureBooking", ...)
req.flash("invalid", ...)
Those will not display because your middleware never reads them.

Instead use:

req.flash("error", "Message sending failed.");
and

req.flash("error", "Failed to send consultation message.");
and

req.flash("error", "All fields are required.");
7. Contact form
Good:

req.flash(
    "success",
    "Message sent successfully."
);

return res.redirect("/auth/contact");
Perfect.

8. Booking
Same here.

Success:

req.flash(
    "success",
    "Consultation request sent successfully."
);
Failure:

req.flash(
    "error",
    "Failed to send consultation request."
);
9. Remove these pages
Once everything has been migrated, these become unnecessary for routine feedback:

exports.failure = ...
exports.success = ...
You can remove those routes and views if nothing else depends on them.

10. One small wording improvement
Instead of:

"User Login successful."
I'd use:

"Welcome back! You have logged in successfully."
or simply:

"Login successful."
It reads more naturally.

Final assessment
You've clearly understood how connect-flash works. The remaining fixes are mostly about consistency:

Use only the flash keys your middleware exposes (success, error, warning).

Redirect after setting a flash instead of rendering failure pages.

Don't pass objects to res.redirect().

Update the signup success message.

Review the logout flow because of req.session.destroy().

Once you apply those changes, your authentication flow will be clean, consistent, 
and in line with the flash notification system you've just built. 
From there, finishing the remaining controllers should be much quicker.


